@SYSTEM_TITLE
; `TOSTR`'s optional second argument is `Int64.ToString(format)`
; (`GameData/Function/Creator.Method.cs:4430-4447`) — the same .NET numeric
; formatter `MONEYSTR` passes its own format to (`:2785`), so erars has one
; implementation for both (`crates/erars-vm/src/dotnet_number.rs`).
; Without a format the value prints as itself.
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(12)}
; A standard specifier is one letter plus an optional precision. `X`/`x` pick
; the case of the hex digits; the precision is a minimum digit count.
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(255,"x")}
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(255,"X6")}
; Custom patterns: `0` is a mandatory digit, `#` an optional one, so the
; leftmost `0` fixes the minimum number of integer digits.
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(12,"000")}
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(42,"0#")}
; Digits never overflow the pattern: extra ones are emitted at the leftmost
; placeholder.
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(1234,"00")}
; `,` between digit placeholders groups by three.
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(1234567,"#,##0")}
; A `'…'` run is a literal, so this `.` is *not* the decimal point: all three
; placeholders hold integer digits and the literal sits between the first and
; the second. This is the form the corpus uses to print a scaled integer.
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(1234,"0'.'00")}
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(12,"0'.'00")}
; `%` scales by 100 and prints itself.
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(12,"0%")}
; Each comma immediately before the (implied) decimal point divides by 1000,
; and the rounding is half away from zero.
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(1234567,"0,,")}
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(1500000,"0,,")}
; Up to three `;` sections: positive, negative, zero. A negative value
; formatted by its own section gets no automatic sign.
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(7,"0;(0);zero")}
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(-5,"0;(0);zero")}
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(0,"0;(0);zero")}
; With no zero section, zero falls back to the first one.
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(0,"0;(0)")}
; One section only: the sign is supplied by the formatter.
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(-5,"000")}
; A letter that is not one of the integral standard specifiers `C D E F G N P X`
; raises `FormatException`, which Emuera reports as `Lang.Error.InvalidFormat`
; naming the method and argument 2 (`Creator.Method.cs:4441-4445`,
; `_Library/EvilMask/Lang.cs:1135`) — the same message `MONEYSTR` gets.
PRINTFORML {TOSTR(1,"Q")}
